<<<
the popular name for the singleword participle that does not end in -ing, i.e., by the p.p. of hear is meant heard, not hearing nor having heard nor being heard. All verbs, with negligible exceptions such as must & can, have this p.p., though in many it is used only as an element in making compound parts like has climbed or will have died. That function of the p.p. is familiar to everyone & needs no comment. Further, the p.p. of all transitive verbs can be used as an adjective (a broken jug).
===
the popular name for the single-word participle that does not end in *-ing*, i.e., by the *p.p.* of *hear* is meant *heard*, not *hearing* nor *having heard* nor *being heard*. All verbs, with negligible exceptions such as *must* & *can*, have this *p.p.*, though in many it is used only as an element in making compound parts like *has climbed* or *will have died*. That function of the *p.p.* is familiar to everyone & needs no comment. Further, the *p.p.* of all transitive verbs can be used as an adjective (*a broken jug*).
>>>

